WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.038
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Privileged Access.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:09.626
This is Chapter 7: access
reviews of privileged roles.

4
00:00:09.626 --> 00:00:14.263
Eligibility only ever grows unless
someone confirms it on a calendar,

5
00:00:14.263 --> 00:00:19.222
so this chapter makes the reviews code:
one file says who reviews which role

6
00:00:19.222 --> 00:00:24.100
and when, a dry run shows the change,
and the decisions come back as evidence.

7
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.536
We'll start from Learn's tutorial
for reviewing administrative roles,

8
00:00:28.536 --> 00:00:31.366
see what it leaves to you,
then walk the series,

9
00:00:31.366 --> 00:00:34.425
the checks, the evidence,
two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:34.425 --> 00:00:39.920
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:44.920
Why reviews at all?

12
00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.407
The approval gate from the earlier
chapters controls when privilege is used.

13
00:00:49.407 --> 00:00:52.407
Reviews control who keeps
the right to use it.

14
00:00:52.407 --> 00:00:55.332
Without them, eligibility only grows.

15
00:00:55.332 --> 00:00:59.685
Book 04 sets the schedule: the
most sensitive tier every month,

16
00:00:59.685 --> 00:01:04.720
the rest every quarter, with access
removed when reviewers stay silent.

17
00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:05.932
And why as code?

18
00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:08.445
Because a review set up by hand drifts.

19
00:01:08.445 --> 00:01:11.193
Someone leaves, a duration gets shortened,

20
00:01:11.193 --> 00:01:14.333
auto-apply is switched off
once and never back on.

21
00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:19.030
In code, every series has a
file, a diff and a reviewer.

22
00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:26.930
Two practical questions next: what
does it cost, and where does it run.

23
00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:30.242
Access reviews belong
to Entra ID Governance.

24
00:01:30.242 --> 00:01:33.776
Learn's tutorial says the P2
license or Governance will do,

25
00:01:33.776 --> 00:01:38.855
but the licensing page asks for Governance
for everyone who reviews or is reviewed,

26
00:01:38.855 --> 00:01:41.505
so the program budgets
for the stricter one.

27
00:01:41.505 --> 00:01:44.852
As for the cloud, this
estate's GCC tenant uses the

28
00:01:44.852 --> 00:01:48.617
worldwide Graph endpoint,
and its Azure is commercial.

29
00:01:48.617 --> 00:01:52.679
The Graph review APIs are listed
for the government clouds too.

30
00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:56.158
The Azure role reviews,
though, run on a preview API,

31
00:01:56.158 --> 00:02:00.160
and their government
availability isn't confirmed.

32
00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:05.622
Here's what Microsoft gives you.

33
00:02:05.622 --> 00:02:09.417
Learn's tutorial walks through
the whole life of one review:

34
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:12.825
create a recurring review
of one administrator role,

35
00:02:12.825 --> 00:02:17.317
list its instances and decisions,
approve one person and deny another,

36
00:02:17.317 --> 00:02:21.422
apply the decisions, pull a history
report, and delete the review.

37
00:02:21.422 --> 00:02:25.259
It also makes a useful point:
one review covers one role.

38
00:02:25.259 --> 00:02:28.262
The requests only exist
inside the web page,

39
00:02:28.262 --> 00:02:33.598
so the repository pins the page's source,
unmodified, with hashes a test checks.

40
00:02:33.598 --> 00:02:36.964
Then the tests send the tutorial's
request to a fake Graph,

41
00:02:36.964 --> 00:02:39.250
so you can see what it really does.

42
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.763
Run against the fake, the tutorial
shows its limits quickly.

43
00:02:46.763 --> 00:02:51.356
It sends a create request every time
without reading first, so run it twice

44
00:02:51.356 --> 00:02:55.875
and you get two reviews with the same
name, and your evidence splits in two.

45
00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:58.295
It names a single person as reviewer,

46
00:02:58.295 --> 00:03:01.763
with no fallback, so one
departure stalls the review.

47
00:03:01.763 --> 00:03:06.404
It sets no default decision and no
auto-apply, so silence keeps privilege,

48
00:03:06.404 --> 00:03:10.350
and a denial changes nothing
until someone applies it by hand.

49
00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:14.900
And its schedule, one day every
three months, isn't the program's.

50
00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:18.790
The code keeps the tutorial's
scope and fixes the rest.

51
00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:25.590
The rest of the gaps are
about evidence and permission.

52
00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.649
The tutorial gives
reviewers no instructions,

53
00:03:28.649 --> 00:03:31.315
though the API marks that field required.

54
00:03:31.315 --> 00:03:35.329
It collects evidence by writing a
report and handing you a link that

55
00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:39.415
expires in a day, and its report may
not even include the review it made.

56
00:03:39.415 --> 00:03:42.783
It never touches Azure roles
or the privileged groups,

57
00:03:42.783 --> 00:03:44.878
two of the program's three targets.

58
00:03:44.878 --> 00:03:48.540
And it uses the write permission
for everything, even reads.

59
00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.830
So the verdict: keep the
tutorial's scope as the model,

60
00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:53.940
but don't run it as published.

61
00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:58.628
Now the code.

62
00:03:58.628 --> 00:04:03.250
It's a new module, and unlike the earlier
chapters, it can write to a tenant,

63
00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:08.341
but only with the apply switch, only what
the dry run listed, and it never deletes.

64
00:04:08.341 --> 00:04:10.590
One file holds every series.

65
00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:14.566
Each has a managed name prefix,
a kind, a tier, a cadence,

66
00:04:14.566 --> 00:04:18.727
reviewers, a fallback and
instructions for reviewers.

67
00:04:18.727 --> 00:04:22.257
The fixture's seven series
follow Book 04's schedule:

68
00:04:22.257 --> 00:04:26.690
role reviews, reviews of the
privileged groups, and one Azure role.

69
00:04:26.690 --> 00:04:31.390
Notice the first row: Global
Administrator makes no change on silence.

70
00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:33.320
We'll see why in a moment.

71
00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:41.107
Each request body is built only
from shapes Learn documents.

72
00:04:41.107 --> 00:04:43.308
A role series reviews users.

73
00:04:43.308 --> 00:04:46.693
The groups that hold the role
aren't reviewed inside it;

74
00:04:46.693 --> 00:04:50.381
each gets its own membership
series, which matches Book 04's

75
00:04:50.381 --> 00:04:54.520
pairing of every role review with
a review of its privileged group.

76
00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:58.545
Group reviews cover the group's
members, a feature still in preview.

77
00:04:58.545 --> 00:05:02.397
Azure role reviews go to Resource
Manager on a preview version,

78
00:05:02.397 --> 00:05:06.620
and their reviewers must be people
or service principals, not groups.

79
00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:10.369
One honest gap: Learn's
request table doesn't list some

80
00:05:10.369 --> 00:05:14.400
fields the code sets, so Lab
2 checks they take effect.

81
00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:21.825
These are the checks the code
runs on every body it builds,

82
00:05:21.825 --> 00:05:24.562
and on any Graph body,
including Learn's own.

83
00:05:24.562 --> 00:05:28.903
Some come straight from Learn: an
empty reviewer list is a self-review,

84
00:05:28.903 --> 00:05:32.863
reviewers need instructions, a
monthly review can't run longer

85
00:05:32.863 --> 00:05:36.975
than twenty-seven days, and Azure
series can't use group reviewers.

86
00:05:36.975 --> 00:05:41.448
Others are the program's design:
auto-apply on, justification required,

87
00:05:41.448 --> 00:05:44.379
the right cadence per
tier, no duplicate names,

88
00:05:44.379 --> 00:05:47.850
and every privileged group
paired with its own review.

89
00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:52.480
One rule, about the emergency
accounts, deserves its own slide.

90
00:05:56.230 --> 00:06:00.857
This is the one place the code deliberately
overrides the program's own setting,

91
00:06:00.857 --> 00:06:03.030
and it ties straight back to Chapter 2.

92
00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:06.705
The emergency accounts hold
Global Administrator permanently.

93
00:06:06.705 --> 00:06:11.831
A monthly review of that role, set to remove
access on silence with auto-apply on,

94
00:06:11.831 --> 00:06:15.617
would quietly remove them the
first month reviewers miss.

95
00:06:15.617 --> 00:06:19.605
Learn itself warns that the
combination can revoke all access.

96
00:06:19.605 --> 00:06:22.550
So that one series makes
no change on silence,

97
00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:27.167
and anything left unreviewed turns
into an evidence finding instead.

98
00:06:27.167 --> 00:06:31.860
Every other series still removes
access when reviewers stay silent.

99
00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:39.916
The dry run reads the tenant first,
matches each series by name,

100
00:06:39.916 --> 00:06:45.235
and compares only what the file sets,
ignoring defaults Graph adds on its own.

101
00:06:45.235 --> 00:06:46.860
Each series gets an action.

102
00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:48.148
New ones are created.

103
00:06:48.148 --> 00:06:52.036
Changed settings are updated in
place, for future reviews only.

104
00:06:52.036 --> 00:06:55.389
But if the scope changes,
Learn's update API won't take it,

105
00:06:55.389 --> 00:06:59.264
so the code marks it for replacement
and leaves that to a person,

106
00:06:59.264 --> 00:07:03.735
because deleting a review deletes its
decisions, and those are evidence.

107
00:07:03.735 --> 00:07:08.160
And the apply refuses to run at
all while the plan has any finding.

108
00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:15.341
Reviews only matter if you
can prove they happened,

109
00:07:15.341 --> 00:07:19.098
so the last piece reads every
decision back, for the ISSO.

110
00:07:19.098 --> 00:07:23.406
Each decision becomes a row: who
was reviewed, what was decided,

111
00:07:23.406 --> 00:07:26.785
by whom, when, and whether
it was actually applied.

112
00:07:26.785 --> 00:07:31.802
Then come the checks an assessor cares
about: a series that's missing or overdue,

113
00:07:31.802 --> 00:07:36.278
a denial that didn't take effect,
silence deciding for a sensitive role,

114
00:07:36.278 --> 00:07:40.214
approvals with no reason, someone
reviewing their own access,

115
00:07:40.214 --> 00:07:44.073
a burst of identical clicks,
and an emergency account denied.

116
00:07:44.073 --> 00:07:47.820
The rows name people, so
treat the folder as evidence.

117
00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:52.632
Who is this for?

118
00:07:52.632 --> 00:07:54.458
Developers build the pipeline.

119
00:07:54.458 --> 00:07:57.958
The identity governance officer
owns the reviews themselves.

120
00:07:57.958 --> 00:08:00.783
And the ISSO reads the results.

121
00:08:00.783 --> 00:08:05.296
The permissions split the same way:
the plan and evidence jobs only read,

122
00:08:05.296 --> 00:08:08.432
so they hold the read
permission and Azure Reader.

123
00:08:08.432 --> 00:08:13.280
Only the apply identity can write, with
User Access Administrator for Azure,

124
00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:15.896
the narrower of the two roles Learn names.

125
00:08:15.896 --> 00:08:19.357
Learn's pages disagree on
which role can create a review,

126
00:08:19.357 --> 00:08:22.820
so the chapter follows the PIM
page and applies as an app.

127
00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:27.220
A test keeps each task's permissions
exactly what its calls need.

128
00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:33.770
Lab 1 runs on a laptop with no tenant.

129
00:08:33.770 --> 00:08:36.820
First, run Learn's
tutorial against the fake.

130
00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.905
Eleven tests pass, and the
third one shows the trap:

131
00:08:39.905 --> 00:08:44.570
send the tutorial's request twice, and
you get two reviews with the same name.

132
00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:47.006
Next, list each task's permissions:

133
00:08:47.006 --> 00:08:51.182
reads for planning and evidence,
writes only for the apply.

134
00:08:51.182 --> 00:08:52.432
Then the dry run.

135
00:08:52.432 --> 00:08:56.354
It finds one series to create,
two to update, one to replace,

136
00:08:56.354 --> 00:09:00.694
three already right, and one
somebody made by hand in the portal.

137
00:09:00.694 --> 00:09:01.870
Nothing is sent.

138
00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:07.000
Only two writes would go out, because the
Azure update needs the preview switch.

139
00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:13.600
The second half of the lab
is where it gets convincing.

140
00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.637
Run the example's checks on
Learn's own request body,

141
00:09:16.637 --> 00:09:21.337
and it fails on the fallback reviewer,
the missing instructions and auto-apply.

142
00:09:21.337 --> 00:09:24.726
Apply in the fake, and only
the two planned writes go out;

143
00:09:24.726 --> 00:09:27.287
everything else is skipped with a reason.

144
00:09:27.287 --> 00:09:30.495
Read the evidence, and every
kind of problem turns up,

145
00:09:30.495 --> 00:09:34.375
from an overdue review to an
emergency account that was denied.

146
00:09:34.375 --> 00:09:38.937
Then try to switch the Global
Administrator series to deny on silence:

147
00:09:38.937 --> 00:09:41.740
the apply refuses, and nothing is sent.

148
00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:50.165
Lab 2 runs one real review,
end to end, in a test tenant.

149
00:09:50.165 --> 00:09:54.015
Pick a role nobody really holds,
never Global Administrator.

150
00:09:54.015 --> 00:09:56.540
Two apps keep reading and writing apart.

151
00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:59.448
You plan as the reader,
then a second person reads

152
00:09:59.448 --> 00:10:02.428
the request bodies before
you apply as the writer.

153
00:10:02.428 --> 00:10:06.241
Then you act as a reviewer:
approve a test user with a reason,

154
00:10:06.241 --> 00:10:08.465
and close the review so it applies.

155
00:10:08.465 --> 00:10:12.465
Read the evidence back, and your
decision is there with its result.

156
00:10:12.465 --> 00:10:15.452
The Azure step is optional,
because it's preview.

157
00:10:15.452 --> 00:10:20.040
To back out, export the evidence
first, then delete by hand.

158
00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:27.978
Each of these dangers is a review
that looks fine and quietly isn't.

159
00:10:27.978 --> 00:10:31.322
The worst ties back to Chapter
2: a setting that removes

160
00:10:31.322 --> 00:10:34.815
the emergency accounts the
first month nobody responds.

161
00:10:34.815 --> 00:10:38.815
Close behind are self-reviews,
reviews with nobody to step in,

162
00:10:38.815 --> 00:10:42.815
and denials that never take
effect because auto-apply is off.

163
00:10:42.815 --> 00:10:47.136
Some are traps of the API itself:
a scope that can't change in place,

164
00:10:47.136 --> 00:10:49.990
or a delete that takes
the evidence with it.

165
00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:52.041
And one the code can't fix alone:

166
00:10:52.041 --> 00:10:56.750
a user denied an Azure role still
sits in the group that grants it.

167
00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:01.850
Here's what done looks like.

168
00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:06.689
Every privileged role, every privileged
group, and every privileged Azure role at

169
00:11:06.689 --> 00:11:09.750
the control-plane subscriptions
has its own series,

170
00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:11.600
and none of them is a self-review.

171
00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:14.658
The cadence matches the
tier, auto-apply is on,

172
00:11:14.658 --> 00:11:19.038
and only the emergency accounts'
series keeps access on silence.

173
00:11:19.038 --> 00:11:23.544
The identities hold only what their
task needs, preview Azure series wait

174
00:11:23.544 --> 00:11:28.125
for the lab, and every closed review
has its decisions in the evidence store.

175
00:11:28.125 --> 00:11:33.987
Next, Chapter 8 brings it all together:
evidence and drift, for the ISSO.

176
00:11:33.987 --> 00:11:35.150
Thanks for watching.
